  ZMQ Middleware
    
    Benefits of ZMQ Middleware:
      Plug-and-play: Add or remove components without breaking others.
      Scalable: Add remote control, new algorithms, or simulators easily.
      Debuggable: Attach a host listener to any ZMQ socket for monitoring.
      Offline-capable: Board logic runs without the host present.
    
   software/zmq directory contains practical example of how you can organize multiple ZeroMQ-based 
   modules running on your Trenz board, to build a flexible, testable, and expandable system
   
      software/zmq/
      ├── zmq_common/              # Shared code (e.g., message structs, utilities)
      ├── sensor_publisher/
      │   ├── src/
      │   ├── CMakeLists.txt
      │   └── build.sh
      ├── data_logger/
      ├── motor_controller/
      ├── navigation_node/
         
    Use systemd services To run your sensor_publisher and data_logger apps automatically at boot.
    Provided example script to  deploy services to board
    
      - Transfers your built binaries (sensor_publisher, data_logger) to the Trenz board

      - Creates systemd .service files and installs them to /etc/systemd/system

      - Enables and starts both services at boot

      - Sets proper executable permissions

      - Cleans up temporary files 
      
    How to build apps and deploy to the board. Apps run automatically at boot.
      1. cd <project_folder>/rover/software/zmq
      2. chmod +x build_libzmq_cross.sh (if not done already)
      3. Run ./build_libzmq_cross.sh
      4. cd <project_folder>/rover/software/<sensor_publisher & data_logger>
      5. chmod +x build.sh (if not done already)
      6. ./build.sh
      7. cd <project_folder>/rover/software/zmq
      8. Power on trenz board
      9. chmod +x deploy_service.sh (if not done already)
      10. Run ./deploy_service.sh
           
